== Economy ==

Ishijima Prefecture's economy is driven by three sectors: volcanic agriculture, geothermal energy, and Shendan maritime logistics. Unlike the heavy manufacturing focus of neighbouring Aizawa, Ishijima Prefecture has developed a complementary economic identity as the agricultural and energy foundation of the eastern Ishijima corridor.

=== Volcanic agriculture ===
The mineral-rich ash soils deposited by Mount Ishijima's periodic eruptions create some of the most fertile agricultural land in Okaiken. The prefecture leverages this volcanic endowment to produce premium tropical crops that command high prices in the metropolitan markets of [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

The signature agricultural product is Ishijima volcanic pepper ({{lang|ja|石島唐辛子}}, ''Ishijima tōgarashi''), a fiery cultivar grown in the ash-enriched soils of the Mount Ishijima foothills. The pepper's distinctive smoky heat—attributed to the high mineral content of the volcanic soil—has made it a sought-after ingredient in Sanese cuisine, particularly in the [[Red Offal Stew]] ({{lang|ja|赤内臓鍋}}, ''Aka-naitō nabe'') that originated in the Aizawa waterfront gymnasiums. The Ishijima Pepper Cooperative, established in 1952, maintains strict quality standards and controls export pricing, generating approximately 円28 billion in annual revenue.

The prefecture also produces significant quantities of sugarcane, tropical mangoes, papayas, and passionfruit, much of it cultivated in geothermally heated greenhouses that extend the growing season year-round. The Ishijima Mango ({{lang|ja|石島マンゴー}}, ''Ishijima Mangō''), grown in the volcanic uplands near Katsuyama, commands premium auction prices comparable to the [[Red Emperor mango]] of [[Miyagi Prefecture]]. The Kiso River delta supports intensive wet-rice cultivation, producing the Ishijima Premium ({{lang|ja|石島特上}}, ''Ishijima Tokujō'') brand sold in luxury markets.

=== Geothermal energy ===
The volcanic activity of both Mount Ishijima and the Asuka Range provides abundant geothermal energy, which powers the prefecture's agricultural infrastructure and supplies surplus electricity to the western Ishijima industrial corridor. The Ishijima Geothermal Complex, the second-largest geothermal installation in Okaiken after the [[Ishiwara Geothermal Complex]], generates 840 MW from 28 production wells tapping the volcanic reservoir beneath the Mount Ishijima caldera.

The geothermal infrastructure also supports the prefecture's extensive onsen (hot spring) network, concentrated in the mountain communities of Okutsu, Nariwa, and Asukaguchi. The [[Dual-Use Hot Spring]] framework established by the Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act of 1994 ensures that geothermal power production maintains the flow rate and temperature of adjacent hot springs, and a portion of geothermal revenues is directed to the local onsen community.

=== Shendan maritime logistics ===
The eastern Shendan coast provides the prefecture with direct ocean access to the trans-Shendan trade routes connecting Okaiken with [[Dagit]], [[Zong]], and the broader Fosian market. The [[Port of Ishijima]], while smaller than the massive Aizawa Port on the western coast, serves as a critical secondary maritime hub for the Ishijima industrial belt, handling approximately 680,000 shipping containers annually. The port specializes in the export of agricultural products and the import of raw materials for the precision engineering districts across the border.

The [[Ghost Fleet]] program's eastern Shendan corridor operates primarily out of Ishijima Port, with autonomous vessels departing for the Dagit and Zong trade lanes. The port's position on the windward side of the island makes it more exposed to typhoon disruption than the sheltered Aizawa harbour, but also provides more direct routing to the eastern Shendan destinations.

=== Fermentation and spirits ===
The volcanic soils and tropical climate of Ishijima Prefecture support a distinctive fermentation tradition. The Ishijima Distillery Cooperative, established in 1898, produces a unique volcanic ''shōchū'' ({{lang|ja|焼酎}}) distilled from sugarcane grown in the ash-enriched soils near Mount Ishijima. The Ishijima Volcanic Shōchū ({{lang|ja|石島火山焼酎}}, ''Ishijima Kazan Shōchū'') is aged in cedar barrels over geothermal heat vents, producing a distinctive smoky flavour profile that has attracted international attention. The 20-year designation commands prices exceeding 円80,000 per bottle in the Okami luxury market.

The prefecture is also known for its volcanic soy sauce, produced by the Ishijima Soy Brewers' Guild using traditional wooden-barrel fermentation methods that leverage the natural warmth of the geothermal soils to accelerate the aging process. The Ishijima Ash Soy ({{lang|ja|石島灰醤油}}, ''Ishijima Hai Shōyu'') brand incorporates trace volcanic minerals into the fermentation, producing a soy sauce with a distinctive earthy depth.
Ishijima's energy and chemical industries are deeply integrated through closed-loop geothermal engineering. The prefecture's volcanic power stations feed baseload electricity into the regional grid while directing superheated thermal discharge into secondary mineral refining plants, which recover high-grade colloidal silica and rare alkali salts from geothermal scaling. The primary ethylene and propylene cracking units located along the coast supply chemical feedstocks directly to Harujima's plastics manufacturing corridor, while volcanic-soil greenhouse cooperatives produce specialty grapes and winter vegetables for metropolitan markets.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Ishijima Prefecture's cuisine is defined by its volcanic terroir and its Shendan maritime heritage. The signature dish is Volcanic Pepper Stew ({{lang|ja|火山辛鍋}}, ''Kazan-kara Nabe''), a ferociously spicy stew of seafood, Ishijima volcanic pepper, and fermented soybean paste, slow-cooked in a stone pot heated by geothermal steam. The dish is a staple of the coastal taverns, where it is consumed as both a meal and a test of endurance.

Other local specialties include Ishijima saba-zushi ({{lang|ja|石島鯖寿司}}, ''Ishijima saba-zushi'')—vinegared mackerel pressed with volcanic rice, a preparation that highlights the ocean-agriculture synthesis of the eastern coast—and Kiso River sweetfish ({{lang|ja|木曽川鮎}}, ''Kiso-gawa Ayu''), grilled over charcoal made from volcanic forest timber.

=== Music ===
The eastern Shendan coast has produced a distinctive musical tradition shaped by the rhythmic patterns of the ocean and the industrial sounds of the geothermal infrastructure. Local musicians have pioneered a genre known as "volcanic bass" ({{lang|ja|火山ベース}}, ''Kazan Bēsu''), which layers deep bass frequencies over recordings of geothermal steam vents and volcanic rumbling. The genre has gained a cult following in the [[Okaiken|Okami]] underground scene and is frequently featured at the annual Ishijima Volcanic Music Festival.

=== The Volcano Festival ===
The annual Ishijima Volcano Festival ({{lang|ja|石島火山祭り}}, ''Ishijima Kazan Matsuri''), held in August, draws over 85,000 visitors to the prefectural capital. The festival features fire dancing, volcanic ash art, geothermal cooking demonstrations, and the ceremonial Chōwa ({{lang|ja|調和}}, "Harmony") ritual, in which community leaders offer the first harvest of volcanic pepper to the mountain—a practice that traces its origins to the pre-Shoto volcanic worship traditions of the eastern coast.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] (Red Line) connects Ishijima Station to [[Aizawa Station]] via the western spine of the island, with onward connections to [[Toyozawa Station]] and [[Okami]]. The Ishijima Eastern Line, a conventional rail service, runs along the Shendan coast connecting the prefectural capital to the southern communities near the Asuka Range.

=== Air ===
Ishijima Airport (ISJ) serves as the regional hub for domestic flights, with connections to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]]. The airport's position on the windward coast means it is frequently disrupted by typhoon conditions during the monsoon season.

=== Maritime ===
The [[Port of Ishijima]] handles approximately 680,000 shipping containers annually and serves as the eastern terminus for the Shendan trade routes. Regular ferry services connect Ishijima to the [[Ogasawara Islands]] and to ports along the Shendan coast of Dagit.

== Education ==

Ishijima Prefecture is home to the Ishijima Institute of Volcanic Agriculture, one of the premier agricultural research universities in Okaiken, specializing in volcanic soil management and tropical crop development. The Ishijima Geothermal Research Center, affiliated with the [[Aizawa Institute of Technology]], conducts research on enhanced geothermal systems and volcanic engineering.

